            <ArticleTitle>[Mesenteric infarct in primary antiphospholipid antibody syndrome].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>669-72</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The mesenteric infarction is a rare but life threatening cause of acute abdomen. A 55-year-old woman was referred to the hospital because of acute mesenteric infarction and in the history claudication. In the absence of risk factors (atrial fibrillation, atherosclerosis, nicotin abusus) a postoperative work up was started to identify the cause of the arterial occlusions. A primary antiphospholipid-antibody syndrome was found. The patient is now receiving low-dose aspirin and anticoagulation therapy. The follow-up over now 14 months shows no further events.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
